Dataset rlp_sales_dataset {
  label: 'RLP Sales (CF-13963)'
  description: '''
    Reproduction of the teveo RLP question (CF-13963): dim_roles ---> fct_sales <--- dim_influencers.
    `h_role` stands in for country so permissions are easy to verify with a real user attribute.

    THE OR RULE
    `rlp_fct_sales.has_role_access` holds the OR itself (destination role = h_role OR influencer target role
    = h_role) with the user attribute baked in, so the RLP rule is a plain `is true`. Exact equality, so no
    concat/contains and no Congo / DR Congo substring risk.

    WHY THE INFLUENCER DIM IS NOT JOINED HERE
    A rule on a joined dim propagates dim -> fact and would drop sale 2 (admin destination / analyst
    influencer), which the OR rule must keep. rlp_propagation cannot stop that: its values are
    'inherit' / 'one_way' / 'two_way', and 'one_way' means one-side -> many-side, i.e. dim -> fact.
    There is no "off". Propagation travels along relationships, so the join is what has to go, not the setting.

    Leaving a joined-but-un-ruled influencer dim in the dataset is NOT an option: a user could ignore any
    restricted copy and query the un-ruled model directly, seeing all influencers. `hidden: true` and omitting
    a model from `view` do not fix that - the AML reference states `hidden` is not a security feature, and
    `view` is an allowlist for the exploration UI.

    So the influencer dim is not joined at all. Instead:
    - Influencer identity in sales analysis comes from `rlp_fct_sales.influencer_name`, which applies
      column-level redaction ('(redacted)' outside the user's role) in the field definition itself. Rows are
      never removed, so the OR rule is unaffected.
    - `rlp_dim_influencers` sits in this dataset with NO relationship and its own `is_my_role` rule, giving the
      restricted "who are my influencers" list. No join path, so its rule cannot reach the fact table.

    Result - both requirements in one dataset, with no un-ruled influencer model anywhere:
    - "see in fct"    -> sale 2 survives
    - "filter in dim" -> directory shows only the user's own influencers

    Trade-off by design: `rlp_dim_influencers` cannot be grouped/filtered together with sales (no join path).
    For per-influencer sales analysis use `rlp_fct_sales.influencer_name` / `influencer_id` on the fact.

    Note: `rlp_dim_roles` is intentionally left un-ruled and joined - the role/country list is not sensitive.
    If it were, it would need the same treatment.
  '''
  data_source_name: 'demodb'

  models: [
    rlp_fct_sales,
    rlp_dim_roles,
    rlp_dim_influencers, // deliberately unjoined, see above
  ]

  relationships: [
    relationship(rlp_fct_sales.role_id > rlp_dim_roles.id, true),
  // no relationship to rlp_dim_influencers: that is what keeps its RLP away from the fact table
  ]

  permission sales_role_access {
    field: r(rlp_fct_sales.has_role_access)
    operator: 'is'
    value: true
  }

  permission influencer_role_access {
    field: r(rlp_dim_influencers.is_my_role)
    operator: 'is'
    value: true
  }

  view {
    model rlp_fct_sales {}
    model rlp_dim_roles {}
    model rlp_dim_influencers {}
  }
}
